{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789997870,"nonce":"0wuVk+uTTKkL8dZ1loQMvFp1ekQ=","title":"A try whose body only throws was typed Void; a return inside a try left the function void; a constructor without super() skipped the ancestors' defaults; an Array\u003cDynamic\u003e element was read as an Int","message":"A try whose body only throws was typed Void; a return inside a try left the function void; a constructor without super() skipped the ancestors' defaults; an Array\u003cDynamic\u003e element was read as an Int\n\nFrom the Issue3948 / Issue4134 / Issue4557 / Issue3799 crash rows (probes TH1-6, DD1, DA1-2).\n\n1. `return try { throw b; } catch (b:Bool) \"ok:\" + b;` — the try\n   expression took the try block's type, Void when the block only throws,\n   so the function had no value to return (\"Return with no value\" and a\n   trap stub). The type is now a catch body's when the try block is Void.\n\n2. `function f(a:PA) { try { throw a; } catch (e:PA) { return \"pa\"; } }` was\n   typed `-\u003e void`: a try in statement position lowers as an expression\n   statement, and the return search knew Return, Block and Conditional\n   expressions only. It now enters the try body and catch bodies. The\n   symptom was a silent exit: the caller concatenated the void result.\n\n3. `class C extends B { new() {} }` with `class A { var ghost = new Ghost(..) }`\n   two levels up: with no `super()` written (none is required when the\n   parent declares no constructor), A's declaration-site defaults never ran\n   and `c.ghost` was null. A constructor without a super call now makes an\n   implicit one, and the super-call lowering walks the ancestry for the\n   nearest class that has a constructor (synthesised for defaults or\n   explicit); a chain with none is a no-op instead of an error.\n\n4. `arr[1]` on an `Array\u003cDynamic\u003e` gave an I64 register (the raw slot), so\n   `\"a \" + arr[1]` printed the box's address and `arr[1] + arr[2]` added\n   addresses. A Dynamic element is a box: the read casts the slot to the\n   box pointer type.\n\nOpen: `var dc:Dynamic\u003cArray\u003cDynamic\u003e\u003e = {a: [0]}; dc.a[0]` reads null and\n`dc.a[0] = 1` crashes -- indexing through a Dynamic-typed field, and an\n`Array\u003cInt\u003e` literal reached through Dynamic holds raw elements (a957716).\n\nFixture: haxe-conformance/regressions/TryExpressionsAndInheritedInits.hx.","files":null}]}